== Mathematical Origin ==

=== Van der Pol Equation for ψ ===

The nonlinear oscillator equation derived from the ψ master equation with a DC pump and phenomenological nonlinear damping:

:<math>\ddot\psi - \mu(1 - \alpha\psi^2)\dot\psi + \omega_0^2\psi = 0</math>

where <math>\mu</math> is the small-signal gain (positive: above threshold) and <math>\alpha</math> controls the nonlinear saturation. The parameter <math>\mu</math> is related to the master equation coupling: <math>\mu \propto \lambda|\psi_{\rm pump}|^2 - \omega\tan\delta_\psi</math>. Above the threshold pump amplitude: <math>\mu > 0</math>, and a limit cycle emerges.

=== Limit Cycle Solution ===

By averaging over the fast oscillation, the limit cycle amplitude:

:<math>\boxed{A_{\rm LC} = \frac{2}{\sqrt{\alpha}} \approx \frac{2}{\sqrt{\lambda_{\rm eff}/\omega_0}}}</math>

The oscillation frequency is shifted slightly from the natural frequency by the nonlinearity:

:<math>\omega_{\rm osc} = \omega_0\left(1 - \frac{\mu^2}{8\omega_0^2}\right) \approx \omega_0</math>

For small <math>\mu</math>, the frequency shift is negligible — the oscillation frequency is essentially the resonant frequency of the underlying [[ψ Resonator]] (C1).

=== Hopf Bifurcation ===

As the pump amplitude increases from zero, the system undergoes a Hopf bifurcation at the threshold where <math>\mu = 0</math>:

* Below threshold (<math>\mu < 0</math>): all perturbations decay → ψ = 0 is the only stable solution.
* At threshold (<math>\mu = 0</math>): undamped oscillation; amplitude grows without bound (linear growth).
* Above threshold (<math>\mu > 0</math>): stable limit cycle at amplitude <math>A_{\rm LC}</math>.

The bifurcation is supercritical (smooth onset) — amplitude grows as <math>\sqrt{\mu - \mu_{\rm th}}</math> above threshold, not as a sudden jump.

=== Injection Locking ===

When a weak external ψ signal at frequency <math>\omega_{\rm inj}</math> (close to <math>\omega_0</math>) is injected into the oscillator, the oscillator can lock to the injected frequency. The locking bandwidth (Adler's equation for ψ):

:<math>|\Delta\omega_{\rm lock}| = |\omega_{\rm inj} - \omega_0| \leq \frac{\mu A_{\rm inj}}{2A_{\rm LC}}</math>

Larger injected amplitude → larger locking bandwidth. Injection locking is the mechanism by which the [[Psi Harmonizer]] phase-locks multiple operators' individual ψ oscillators to a common shared reference — even if each operator's natural frequency differs slightly, injection from the shared field locks them all within <math>\Delta\omega_{\rm lock}</math>.

== Implementation: Psi Harmonizer Oscillator Stage ==

The [[Psi Harmonizer]] oscillator operates as follows:

# Each operator's [[Psi Stabilizer]] is in Anchor mode, establishing a ψ field at their individual coherence frequency <math>\omega_{\rm bio}^{(i)}</math>.
# The Harmonizer shell provides a high-Q [[ψ Resonator]] (C1) at a common reference frequency <math>\omega_{\rm ref}</math>.
# Above the threshold of N aligned operators, the collective ψ field exceeds the Van der Pol threshold — the oscillator locks.
# Injection locking then pulls each operator's individual frequency toward <math>\omega_{\rm ref}</math> within the locking bandwidth.
# Once locked, the collective field grows via the [[ψ Amplifier]] (C7) N⁴ mechanism.

The Harmonizer oscillator and amplifier stages are coupled — the oscillator provides the frequency reference; the amplifier provides the gain. They are implemented in the same resonator cavity, with the oscillator stage operating at the fundamental and the amplifier stage at the second harmonic.

== ψ-Dependent Extensions ==

The biological ψ oscillator: Within Track B, trained operators sustain a limit-cycle ψ oscillation in their cortical tissue, where the biological <math>\lambda_{\rm eff}^{\rm (bio)}</math> plays the role of the nonlinear coefficient and coherent neural synchrony provides the threshold-crossing condition. The Van der Pol model predicts that trance, flow, and meditation states correspond to above-threshold limit-cycle operation — the sustained psionic state is self-maintaining rather than effortful because the nonlinear gain compensates the biological loss. This is the mathematical basis for the phenomenological distinction between "forced" and "natural" psionic states reported by practitioners.

